You can go back over all the old posts we got with advice,
but I think lsoda was the one that deSolve uses in R (albeit
the original Fortran implementation).  I believe it computes
Jacobians with finite differences dynamically on an as-needed
basis.

My only concern with lsoda is the translation quality from Fortran.
I know other work by the translator, so have every reason to believe
it'll be reasonable.

I don't really know anything about cvode.  How does it take
Jacobians?  There's the much longer-about way that Michael
worked out the math for and requires higher-order autodiff (which
we conveniently now have).  I'm not sure if that'll be faster in
the end, but probably not if it has to compute the Jacobian every
step, other than for problems where that's required.

- Bob

